"""INA229HighSideMonitor: reusable high-side INA229 power/energy monitor with an on-block Kelvin shunt.

Block: INA229AIDGS (85 V, 20-bit power/energy/charge monitor, SPI) sensing a WSK2512 4-terminal current-sense
shunt between BUS_IN and BUS_OUT. Positive current flows BUS_IN -> BUS_OUT. The shunt voltage taps (E1, E2) feed
IN+ and IN- directly, so the trace resistance outside the shunt is not measured. The INA229 VBUS (bus voltage)
input is wired inside the block to BUS_OUT, the low side of the shunt (DS Fig 10-1 note 1, load power).
High-side sensing only: with VBUS tied to BUS_OUT the block cannot be used as a low-side monitor.

io: BUS_IN, BUS_OUT (shunt current path), V3V3 (2.7 V to 5.5 V accepted, per the INA229 rec-op; the board supplies
the 3.3 V logic rail), GND, SPI (Spi: CS, MISO, MOSI, CLK; CS active low), ALERT_N (active low, open drain).

config: rshunt (Resistance, no default, set by the board), rshunt_tol (shunt tolerance code, default "B"),
alert_pullup (bool, default True), ambient (optional Temperature range), derate (float, default 1.0).

Instances: U (INA229), SHUNT (WSK2512), C_VS (100 nF decoupling at VS), R_MISO (33 ohm source termination),
R_ALERT (10 kohm ALERT_N pull-up, DNP when a host or shared wired-OR pull-up is used). Topology does not depend on
configs: configs change values and dnp only.

Schematic: a sub-sheet (its own page, child file named after the instance in the board's eda/). The arranged
reference sheet is benches/INA229HighSideMonitor/eda/INA.kicad_sch; boards stamp it per instance with
tools/schematic/stamp_block.py.

Values: rshunt comes from this block's analysis.py, run per instance (worst case over the zener-library limits). The 33 ohm MISO
termination is a placeholder DESIGN CHOICE (R_out not yet known, see spec.md open items). The 10 kohm ALERT pull-up
and 100 nF VS decoupling are from DS Fig 8-2 and DS section 9.
"""

load("@stdlib/interfaces.zen", "Spi")
load("@stdlib/units.zen", "Impedance", "Resistance", "Temperature", "Voltage")

Project(name="INA229HighSideMonitor", path="INA229HighSideMonitor", layout=False)

INA229 = Module("github.com/kb1lqc/zener-library/components/TexasInstruments/INA229AIDGS/INA229AIDGS.zen")
WSK2512 = Module("github.com/kb1lqc/zener-library/components/Vishay/WSK2512/WSK2512.zen")
GCM188 = Module("github.com/kb1lqc/zener-library/components/Murata/GCM188/GCM188.zen")
ERJ3EKF = Module("github.com/kb1lqc/zener-library/components/Panasonic/ERJ-3EKF/ERJ-3EKF.zen")

BUS_IN = io(Net)
BUS_OUT = io(Net)
# Intended rail is 3.3 V (the name); the block accepts 2.7 V to 5.5 V per the INA229 recommended operating range.
# A plain Net so the sub-sheet draws a local hierarchical label (not a global power symbol); the board's V3V3 Power
# net carries the voltage.
V3V3 = io(Net)
# Plain Net for the same reason; the cast below re-types it for library pins that require Ground.
GND = io(Net)
_GND = Ground(GND)  # library parts type ground pins as Ground
# CS is active low.
SPI = io(Spi)
# Active low, open drain.
ALERT_N = io(Net)

rshunt = config(Resistance, help="Shunt resistance, an orderable WSK2512 value for the chosen rshunt_tol")
rshunt_tol = config(
    str,
    default="B",
    help="WSK2512 tolerance code: B (+/-0.1 %, 0.01 to 0.2 ohm), D (+/-0.5 %, 0.001 to 0.2 ohm) or F (+/-1.0 %, 0.0005 to 0.2 ohm); B is the narrowest value range, D and F also reach lower values",
)
alert_pullup = config(bool, default=True, help="Fit the ALERT_N pull-up (R_ALERT); False when the host supplies one")
ambient = config(Temperature, optional=True, help="Board ambient temperature range")
derate = config(float, default=1.0, help="Project derating factor in (0, 1]; shrinks abs-max only")
vsense_max = config(
    Voltage, optional=True, help="Worst-case shunt voltage I_max x R_worst from the analysis; sets the shunt v_across"
)

# The wrappers' optional ambient config rejects None, so pass it only when the board sets it.
_AMB = {"ambient": ambient} if ambient != None else {}
# Shunt v_across from the module's analysis, passed only when the board sets vsense_max.
_VSENSE = {"v_across": vsense_max} if vsense_max != None else {}

# Kelvin sense nets from the shunt taps to the INA229 IN+/IN-. The taps sit on the I1/I2 terminals, so they carry the
# bus voltage; the shunt drop (<= 0.16 V) is negligible versus the declared range. None when the block is built alone.
SENSE_P = Net(voltage=BUS_IN.voltage)
SENSE_N = Net(voltage=BUS_OUT.voltage)
# INA229 MISO output, before the source series resistor: same logic levels as the SPI MISO line it drives.
MISO_SRC = Net(voltage=SPI.MISO.voltage)

U = INA229(
    name="U",
    CS=SPI.CS,
    MOSI=SPI.MOSI,
    SCLK=SPI.CLK,
    MISO=MISO_SRC,
    ALERT=ALERT_N,
    VS=V3V3,
    GND=_GND,
    # Bus-voltage sense from the low side of the shunt (BUS_OUT, the load-side current terminal I2), not the Kelvin
    # tap E2. DS Fig 10-1 note 1: load power.
    VBUS=BUS_OUT,
    IN_P=SENSE_P,
    IN_N=SENSE_N,
    derate=derate,
    **_AMB,
)

SHUNT = WSK2512(
    name="SHUNT",
    value=rshunt,
    tolerance=rshunt_tol,
    I1=BUS_IN,
    I2=BUS_OUT,
    E1=SENSE_P,
    E2=SENSE_N,
    derate=derate,
    **dict(_AMB, **_VSENSE),
)

# Decoupling at the VS pin.
C_VS = GCM188(
    name="C_VS",
    value="100nF",
    rated_v="50V",
    dielectric="X7R",
    tolerance="+/-10%",
    P1=V3V3,
    P2=_GND,
    derate=derate,
    **_AMB,
)

# SPI.MISO side of R_MISO: same electrical net as SPI.MISO, marked 50 ohm.
MISO_LINE = Net(SPI.MISO, impedance=Impedance(50))

# Source series termination at the INA229 MISO pin. DESIGN CHOICE: 33 ohm is a placeholder. The INA229 DS bounds
# MISO output resistance only as <= 400 ohm (VOH at 1 mA). R_series + R_out ~= 50 ohm trace impedance; the trace after
# this resistor (MISO_LINE) is 50 ohm controlled impedance.
R_MISO = ERJ3EKF(
    name="R_MISO",
    value="33ohm",
    P1=MISO_SRC,
    P2=MISO_LINE,
    # Voltage across the termination, conservative edge-peak bound: VS_max x R / (R + Z0) = 3.399 V x 33 / (33 + 50)
    # = 1.35 V, assuming driver R_out >= 0 and a 50 ohm line. DC drop is ~0 (CMOS input load). The rated check uses
    # this instead of the two nets' ranges.
    v_across="1.35V",
    derate=derate,
    **_AMB,
)

# ALERT_N pull-up to V3V3 (DS Fig 8-2). DNP when the host or a shared wired-OR pull-up is used.
R_ALERT = ERJ3EKF(
    name="R_ALERT",
    value="10kohm",
    P1=ALERT_N,
    P2=V3V3,
    dnp=not alert_pullup,
    derate=derate,
    **_AMB,
)
